Looking at the project snapshot, I need to identify the highest-risk restore consistency bug related to boot-time restore exposing an inconsistent running state before threads are restored.

Let me analyze the `restoreSession()` method in `GameRuntimeServer.ts`:

```typescript
private async restoreSession(): Promise<void> {
  const saved = await this.stateStore.load();
  if (!saved) {
    return;
  }

  const content = this.contentStore.getContent();
  if (saved.simulation) {
    this.simulation = Simulation.fromState(saved.simulation, content);
    this.seed = saved.simulation.seed;
    for (const agent of saved.simulation.agents ?? []) {
      this.agentConfigs.set(agent.id, { id: agent.id, name: agent.name });
    }
    this.resetAgentLoopState((saved.simulation.agents ?? []).map((agent) => agent.id));
  } else if (typeof saved.seed === "number") {
    // ...
  } else {
    return;
  }

  this.phase = "running";
  this.preparedSeed = null;
  this.threadIds = { gameplayByAgentId: {} };
  this.threadToAgentId.clear();
  this.activeTurn = null;
  this.schedulerIndex = 0;
  await this.logReplay("session.restored", {
    seed: this.seed,
    tick: this.simulation.getState().tick
  });
}
```

The critical issue: **`restoreSession()` sets `this.phase = "running"` BEFORE restoring the threads from the saved session.** 

Looking at the saved `SessionSnapshot` type:
```typescript
export type SessionSnapshot = {
  seed: number;
  tick: number;
  threadIds: ThreadIds;  // <-- This is saved!
  metrics: RuntimeMetrics;
  simulation: SimulationState;
};
```

The `threadIds` are persisted in the snapshot but never restored in `restoreSession()`. This means:


- `this.threadIds` gets reset to an empty object
- `this.threadToAgentId` gets cleared
- Yet `this.phase` is set to `"running"`

This creates a dangerous inconsistency where the scheduler and other logic will attempt to use threads that don't exist yet. When `runScheduler()` executes, it tries to access `this.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId[agent.id]` which will be empty, and `requestGameplayTurn()` will fail silently or error because no valid thread exists.

The real problem is that `restoreSession()` restores the simulation state but not the thread mappings. The solution is to restore `threadIds` from the saved snapshot and rebuild the `threadToAgentId` map, then verify the threads are actually available (either by resuming existing ones or creating new ones if they're stale).

Looking at the code flow, `primeCodexModelCatalog()` tries to start threads only if `threadIds` are empty, but since `restoreSession()` already sets `phase = "running"`, the scheduler can run before threads are properly initialized. The fix should restore the saved `threadIds` and `threadToAgentId` mappings, then attempt to resume the threads rather than creating new ones.

The critical issue is that `restoreSession()` marks the session as running without verifying the threads actually exist. If the threads were from a previous process, they're likely stale and won't respond. I need to restore the thread IDs from the snapshot, rebuild the agent-to-thread mapping, and either resume existing threads or create fresh ones—without prematurely setting the phase to running until the threads are confirmed active. The core issue is that `restoreSession()` sets `phase = "running"` before threads are actually restored, which allows the scheduler to execute prematurely. I need to restore the saved `threadIds` and `threadToAgentId` mappings first, then ensure threads are resumed or created before transitioning to the running phase.

The fix involves restoring the thread mappings from the snapshot, then calling `startThreadsForExistingSession()` to handle thread restoration. However, I should verify this method doesn't create duplicate threads by checking if the thread IDs already exist in the saved state before attempting to create new ones. I need to reconsider the approach. Looking at `startThreadsForExistingSession()`, it unconditionally creates new threads via `thread/start` requests without checking if threads already exist. This means restoring from a snapshot would create duplicate threads instead of resuming the existing ones.

The real fix is to restore the thread IDs from the snapshot and attempt to resume them first, only creating new threads if the resume fails. I should modify `restoreSession()` to preserve the saved `threadIds` and `threadToAgentId` mappings, then add logic to attempt resuming those threads before falling back to thread creation.

